= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The first block is the <companion matrix> of $t-3$. The last block is the <companion matrix> of $f(t)=t^3-3t^2+9t-27=(t-3)(t^2+9)$. Since $t-3$ divides $f$, these are the ordered <invariant factors of a linear operator>. Thus
$$
\boxed{m_\alpha(t)=(t-3)(t^2+9),\qquad\chi_\alpha(t)=(t-3)^2(t^2+9).}
$$
The <minimal polynomial> is the largest invariant factor, and the <characteristic polynomial> is their product.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <minimal polynomial> factors over $\mathbb C$ as $(t-3)(t-3i)(t+3i)$ with distinct roots. The <diagonalizability criterion using the minimal polynomial> therefore makes $\alpha$ diagonalizable. Its <characteristic polynomial> gives multiplicities two, one and one, so its <Jordan normal form> is
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{diag}(3,3,3i,-3i),}
$$
up to reordering the one-dimensional <Jordan blocks>.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

For a two-by-two matrix over a <principal ideal domain>, its first <Smith normal form> invariant is the <greatest common divisor> of its entries, while the product of its two invariants is its <determinant>, up to a unit. Here
$$
1=3-(1-i)(1+i),
$$
so the entries generate the unit ideal in the <Gaussian integers>. The first invariant is therefore $1$. Also
$$
\det B=3(1+i)-(2+2i)(5+2i)=-3-11i.
$$
Hence a valid <Smith normal form> is
$$
\boxed{\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&-3-11i\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
Multiplying the second diagonal entry by a unit $\pm1,\pm i$ gives an equivalent normal form. The divisibility condition holds because $1$ divides every <Gaussian integer>.

