= Solution
{parent=i}

**True.** Every <group> of order four is <Abelian>: if it has an element of order four it is cyclic; otherwise every nonidentity element has order two and $(ab)^{-1}=b^{-1}a^{-1}=ba$, while $(ab)^{-1}=ab$, so $ab=ba$.

Over $\mathbb C$, the <Maschke theorem> and the <Artin-Wedderburn theorem> give
$$
\mathbb CG\cong\prod_{j=1}^rM_{d_j}(\mathbb C).
$$
Since $G$ is <Abelian>, this <algebra> is commutative, forcing every $d_j=1$. Its <dimension> is four, so $r=4$. Each factor contributes one simple <module>; therefore there are exactly **four** up to <isomorphism>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**True.** If every <simple module> over $\mathbb CG$ were one-dimensional, the <Maschke theorem> and the <Artin-Wedderburn theorem> would make $\mathbb CG$ a product of copies of $\mathbb C$, hence commutative. But its <basis> elements multiply as in $G$, so commutativity of $\mathbb CG$ implies commutativity of $G$. This contradicts the assumption. Therefore a non-<Abelian> finite <group> has a <simple module> of <dimension> greater than one.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

**True.** The polynomial $X^7-1$ has seven distinct roots $\zeta_7^j$ in $\mathbb C$. The <Chinese remainder theorem> gives
$$
\mathbb C[X]/(X^7-1)\cong\prod_{j=0}^6\mathbb C.
$$
The simple <modules> are exactly the seven one-dimensional evaluation <modules>, on which $X$ acts by $\zeta_7^j$. Thus there are **seven** up to <isomorphism>.

= iv
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

**True; there are exactly five.** On a simple <module>, the central element $Y$ acts as zero: its <image> is a <submodule>, and surjectivity would contradict $Y^6=0$. The <nilpotent central elements annihilate simple modules> argument shows that the action factors through
$$
\mathbb C[X,Y]/(X^5-1,Y)\cong\mathbb C[X]/(X^5-1)\cong\mathbb C^5
$$
by the <Chinese remainder theorem>. Therefore the simple <modules> are one-dimensional with
$$
\boxed{X\mapsto\zeta_5^j,\qquad Y\mapsto0,\qquad j=0,\ldots,4.}
$$
This also rules out hypothetical infinite-dimensional simple <modules>, rather than counting only finite-dimensional ones.

